WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:12.190
ADAM MICHELSON: Hi, and
welcome to our Cloud Next

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.690
session on load balancers.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.190
And today, we're
going to be talking

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:18.700
about the next generation
of our Cloud load balancers.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.110
We've introduced many
new features recently.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.320
And we're excited today
to share with you some

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.750
of what those features entail.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.430
But first, let's talk--

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.920
let me present to
you our presenters.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.560
So Adam Michelson--
I'm a product

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.440
manager at Google
Cloud, and I help

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.480
run the Cloud load balancers.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.880
Babi, do you want to
introduce yourself?

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:40.880
BABI SEAL: Hi, everyone.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:41.470
I'm Babi.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.230
I'm also a product manager
on the load balancing team.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.030
Thank you, Adam.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.030
ADAM MICHELSON: Awesome.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.190
So with no further ado,
let's dive right in.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.890
So today, we're going to talk
to you about three items.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.910
One is next-generation
load balancing.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.210
We've been working to
re-platform many of our load

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.530
balancers to include advanced
traffic control capabilities.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.110
Our load balancers have
been re-platformed to run

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.440
on top of the Envoy
open source system.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.290
And if you're familiar
with the Envoy system,

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.030
then many of these features
may be familiar to you.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:15.770
And if you're not,
that's totally fine.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.410
You don't need to
know that platform

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.240
to use our Cloud load balances.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.560
We'll also be talking about
how using this common platform

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.550
will enable you to have common
capabilities across our load

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.880
balancers as well as common
control planes across our load

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.230
balancers.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.990
Next, we'll also be talking
about load balancing

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:35.750
everywhere.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.740
This is a feature where, if
you have services that run off

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.650
of Google Cloud, such as
on-prem or in a multi-cloud

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.320
environment, how you can
connect to those services

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.290
through our load balancers--
so if you run services that

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.300
are not running on
Google, you can still

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.850
access those services
via our load balancers.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.010
And finally, we're going
to talk to you about some

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.870
of our enterprise-ready
features,

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.140
such as Private Service Connect,
which enables you to connect

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.680
to third-party-- or services
that are not necessarily

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.810
under your
organization's control,

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.330
as well as how you can connect
to third-party applications.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.389
So first, we're going
to talk about some

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:13.700
of our next-generation load
balancing capabilities.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.270
So for the folks who may not
be familiar with our load

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.900
balancers, this is a quick
overview of our load balancers.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.390
We have a few load balancers
in our load balancer fleet.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.050
We have both a set of Level 7
HTPS load balancers as well as

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.450
Level 4 network load balancers.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.570
Most of the next-generation
capabilities

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.440
you're going to see in our
Level 7 load balancers.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.980
So as we were saying,
many of our load balancers

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.640
are getting an upgrade, where
we have advanced traffic control

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.010
capabilities brought by Envoy.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:52.270
Now, Envoy, as I said earlier,
is an open source platform.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.820
If you're familiar with
the Envoy platform, then,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.280
again, these services
may be familiar to you.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.460
One of the commitments Google
has is to the open source

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:01.340
community.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.400
And this is an
example of that, where

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.480
we work with the Envoy community
to contribute features back

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:08.470
to that community.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:10.210
And then we consume
those features

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.090
into these load balances.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.890
So as part of that
capability, you'll

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:18.760
see our load balancer have
consistent set of features,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.850
also consistent with the
open source capabilities.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.010
And through that
consistency, you'll

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:27.550
see common control planes,
common observability,

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.140
and common feature set
throughout our load balances.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.020
During the initial release
of our load balancers

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:35.880
that support our
Envoy, you'll have--

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.740
you'll see many new features.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.860
Here on the right, you'll see
examples of what those are.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.660
I'm not going to go
through all of them,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.500
but I'll call out a couple.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.250
So one example is weighted
traffic splitting.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.860
Weighted traffic splitting
is used when you roll out,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.530
for example, a new
feature or a new service

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.390
and you want to
test that service,

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.695
such as running a canary
test across those services.

00:03:57.695 --> 00:03:59.070
Weighted traffic
splitting allows

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.620
you to take a certain
percentage of your traffic--

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.190
1%, 2%, 5%, 50%, what
have you-- and push it

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.690
to the new service
you're rolling out.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.260
Another example is
fault injection.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.530
In this example,
you can emulate what

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.649
would happen if you had
a fault in your system

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.329
before it happens in production.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.640
That way, you can
harden your deployment

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.750
to make sure that when errors do
inevitably occur in production,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.030
you've had an opportunity
to test them using

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.460
fault injection, as an example.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.010
Now, this advanced
traffic control capability

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.980
of our load balancer actually
extends beyond just load

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.170
balancer capabilities.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.560
For those who, again, may be
familiar with using something

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.680
like an Envoy sidecar, this
example may be familiar to you.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.380
For those who are
not, Envoy, again,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.350
is an open source component
and platform which

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.300
handles traffic management.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.730
We have some of those
capabilities built natively

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.080
into our load balancers.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.170
And again, you don't have
to understand Envoy at all

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:02.590
to use our load balancers.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.160
They're just native features
of our load balances.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.880
But for deployments that
are heterogeneous by nature,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.620
multicloud, or on-premises,
and you want a common feature

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.580
set of traffic control
across Cloud and on-premises

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.450
or multicloud and you want
common policy management

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.670
and control, you
can deploy what's

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.830
called an Envoy
sidecar, which is

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.200
a deployable piece of
code that can deploy next

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.460
to your services.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.490
And those sidecar will contain
traffic control capabilities.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.430
And if you wish, you
can use a component,

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:37.830
such as Google's
Traffic Director, which

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.210
is a managed control plane that
can push XDS configurations

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:44.640
and controls into your sidecars.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.290
And it doesn't have to be Envoy.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:50.230
It just has to be an
XDS-compatible service.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:54.390
So if you do that, then you
can have some services sit

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.730
behind Google Cloud
load balancers

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:01.530
and other services that have
a sidecar sitting next to them

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.090
in Google Cloud or
on-premises or in multicloud.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:09.810
And in that architecture, you
can handle a highly distributed

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:13.115
set of systems running
across heterogeneous systems,

00:06:13.115 --> 00:06:14.490
but running as if
they're running

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.280
in a homogeneous environment
because they're all

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.790
standardized using the Envoy
traffic control capabilities.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:22.890
They'll have common features.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.660
They'll have common
control planes.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.000
They'll have common
observability.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.800
Let's run through
a quick example

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.970
of how this might be useful.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.320
So in this case, imagine
you're an e-commerce provider

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:37.950
and you have a shared service
across many components--

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:41.250
in this case, a wallet service
that could be maybe a payment

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.690
processing engine.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.390
Now, here we have
an e-commerce engine

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:48.660
that will use the external
global load balancer

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.460
to access that service.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.580
We also have,
potentially, a call center

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.970
using an internal load balancer,
which is, again, hitting

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.267
the same common service.

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:00.850
And on the right
side of this picture,

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:03.750
you'll see the tension is point
of sale using regional load

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.900
balancers, or maybe there's
nightly batch jobs running

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.880
on-premises, maybe
running fraud protection.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.260
So in all these cases,
you have a common service

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.955
that these various
systems are using.

00:07:15.955 --> 00:07:17.580
So what happens if
you want to roll out

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.940
a new service, a
version 2 of the wallet?

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.250
Each of these
disparate systems need

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.980
to be able to test that
service using something

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.150
like a canary test.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.930
That's pretty complicated to
do if each of these systems

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.437
has its own capabilities.

00:07:32.437 --> 00:07:34.770
And some may or may not have
weighted traffic splitting,

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:35.940
for example, built in.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:41.490
But if you're using something
like an Envoy platform

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:45.600
everywhere, then you can use a
common weighted traffic system

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.470
across all these systems.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:51.300
This will be the same
capability, the same control

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.500
planes, the same observability
to see if features are working

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:56.950
or if they're not working.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:01.650
And in this way, you can go
ahead and test a secondary

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.870
wallet service--
say, version 2--

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.750
and see if that service
is working as expected.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.060
And once it is, then
you can push all traffic

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.610
to that new service.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:14.010
And so in this way, if you
do have a highly distributed

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.530
set or a complicated
architecture that

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.750
has reusable
services, whether it

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:22.110
be just a distributed set
of services or a system

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.250
like service mesh, you can use
advanced traffic management

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.990
that's built natively
into our load

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:32.220
balancers and other
components to reduce the toil

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.429
and overhead of managing
a highly distributed

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:36.710
set of services.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:40.590
So now what I want to do is
run you through a quick demo

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.230
to see this live in action.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.420
In this demonstration,
we're going

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.940
to see how to roll
out a canary test.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.250
Here, we see four
different services--

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.500
in the upper left, a
global load balancer,

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.530
lower left-- regional load
balancer, upper right--

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.240
an internal load balancer,
and in the lower right,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.180
we see a sidecar proxy.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.100
Here we're rolling out
the various commands

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.460
required to be able
to do our canary test.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:08.700
Now we're seeing that some
traffic is switching over

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.840
from version 1 to version 2.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.100
And over the course of
about 30 seconds or so,

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.460
all the various traffic
management solutions,

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.800
load balancer and
sidecar proxies,

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:23.160
are beginning to send their
traffic to both version

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:24.990
1 and version 2.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.990
The purpose is to make sure that
version 2 is handling traffic

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:29.040
properly.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.890
At this point, we see a lot
of traffic going to version 2.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.230
So now we're going to say that
everything is working properly.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:37.680
And we're going to
send all of our traffic

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:40.410
now to the version 2
and away from version 1

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.120
and do our full rollout.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:45.060
So again, we have the
same four services.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.430
And we're going to run
the commands, which

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.890
are going to be common across
these various services,

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:55.650
to be able to switch our traffic
onto the new, updated version 2

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:57.240
wallet service.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.030
And once again, it takes
about 30 seconds or so

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.700
for all the services
to get the command

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.490
and to switch all their
traffic over to version 2.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.040
And so now you see that all
the traffic at this point

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.540
has been switched to version 2.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.040
And our canary
rollout is complete.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.430
Now that you've seen how
the next generation of load

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:19.120
balancers can enable advanced
traffic management capabilities

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.910
across a highly distributed
set of services,

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.410
I want to turn the
conversation over

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:26.440
to Babi, who is going
to run you through some

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.490
of our capabilities around
load balancing everywhere.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:30.970
Babi?

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:34.090
BABI SEAL: So one of the goals
of our Cloud load balancing

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:37.640
is to meet you where you're
at in your journey to Cloud.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.390
And as part of that, we have
gone ahead and introduced

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:42.350
hybrid load balancing.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:45.890
So what is hybrid
load balancing?

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:51.230
You have clients that could be
external to Google or internal.

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:55.370
They send traffic to our
external HTTPS load balancing

00:10:55.370 --> 00:11:01.360
or external TCP proxy or our
internal HTPS load balancing.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.840
These load balances have
back ends, of course,

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:06.070
on Google Cloud.

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:10.360
But now we've enhanced them
to also support back ends

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:14.200
on-prem and in other clouds.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.930
These on-prem and
other cloud back ends

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:21.340
are connected to Google
either via public connectivity

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:26.080
across the internet or
via private connectivity

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.090
through HA VPN or interconnects.

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:32.710
What we're really
excited to share

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.490
with you is that we have
now in public preview

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:39.040
our hybrid load
balancing with support

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.620
for on-prem and other Cloud back
ends via private connectivity.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.800
And we support health
checking of these back ends.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:51.080
And we'd really like
for you to try them out.

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.430
So how are some of our customers
using hybrid load balancing?

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.880
What are some of-- a use cases?

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.590
The most
straightforward use case

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.390
is load balancing to
workloads on on-prem.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:07.860
More concretely, you can imagine
an ERP application running

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:14.170
on-prem [INAUDIBLE] regional
extension connected to Google

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:17.410
via partner interconnects.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:20.110
The second use
case is protecting

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:25.570
your on-prem workloads against
DDoS attacks with Cloud Armor,

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:31.000
or you can use identity-aware
proxy to authorize access

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.800
to your on-prem resources.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:38.180
You can enhance the latency
experience for your end users

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:42.450
by caching static
content on CDN.

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:46.290
The third use case uses
weighted traffic splitting

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:49.320
that Adam had alluded
to earlier to do

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:54.840
a gradual migration of workloads
from on-prem to Google.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.020
Now we are working on
additional use cases,

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:04.560
such as supporting failover
from on-prem to Google.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:09.990
As we develop more and more,
we shall share them with you.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:13.620
Now let us turn our
attention to how

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:17.430
we are enhancing our enterprise
users with hybrid load

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.880
balancing use cases.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:24.820
So what we are focused on is
reducing the operational toil

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:28.750
and meeting the scale
and security requirements

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:31.000
for our enterprise users.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.390
In this regard, first let
us look at Private Service

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:35.640
Connect.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:38.220
So what is Private
Service Connect?

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.620
In typical customer
deployments, you

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:44.970
have multiple teams producing
services for consumption

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.270
by other teams.

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:49.010
The groups offering
these services

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:51.380
could be part of the
same organization

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:55.790
or they could be third-party
services or services offered

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:57.870
by Google.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.450
The way you typically
set this up securely

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:03.450
is via private
connectivity by setting up

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:07.670
peering between the
producer and the consumer.

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:11.750
But you run into a lot of
operational toil and scale

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:14.530
constraints with peering.

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:18.510
Now, this is where Private
Service Connect comes in.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:22.410
It is our converged service
connectivity offering.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.920
Because it does this through
private connectivity,

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.290
it is secure.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.300
You don't have the
scale constraints

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.160
that you have with peering.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.550
And it can scale to
thousands of endpoints.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.620
It is also very performant.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.550
You get line rate because there
is no proxy in the middle.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:45.750
Now, how does this Private
Service Connect actually work?

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.140
Well, you have a
producer and a consumer.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.420
And the producer--
you set up the service

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:56.830
behind a load balancer and
a service attachment point

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.760
in the producer's address space.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:03.060
This service attachment
point is linked

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:07.810
to endpoints in the
consumer's address space.

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:10.210
The workloads in the
consumer, wishing

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:14.500
to access the endpoints,
simply send traffic

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:18.590
to the endpoints in
their own address space.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:23.190
So how does load balancing work
with Private Service Connect?

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.880
Let's start with the producer.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:30.140
The internal HTPS load balancer
and the internal Layer 4 load

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:33.050
balancer helps you
scale out your service

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:35.980
and makes it highly available.

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:37.530
Now, remember how
I was referring

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.360
to hybrid load balancing?

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:44.790
The internal HTPS load balancer
now supports endpoints on-prem.

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.730
So you can use the
Private Service Connect

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:52.290
to offer a service
residing on-prem securely

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:58.460
without having to worry about
overlapping IP address space.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:00.770
On the consumer
front, we are now

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:06.980
working on putting these PSC
endpoints behind our HTTP load

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:12.060
balancers, external or internal.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:16.020
With the URL maps that are
associated with these HTP load

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:21.510
balancers, you get fine-grained
control and selection of which

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:25.160
producer services
that you route to.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:29.060
You also can now log
every service invocation

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:32.272
with our HTPD load
balancing logging.

00:16:32.272 --> 00:16:37.940
In addition, for external
services that access HBS load

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:39.520
balancer, you can
put a Cloud Armor

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:44.740
and protect against DDoS attacks
or use our managed TLS offering

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:46.360
to secure the service further.

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:50.860
Now I'm excited
to share with you

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:55.060
another capability that we have
enhanced for our enterprise

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:55.690
users.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:17:00.600
And that is integration
with third-party appliances.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:02.610
So a lot of our
enterprise users,

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:05.280
as they migrate to
Google Cloud, wish

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:08.400
to bring their favorite
third-party appliances

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.609
with them as part of
the lift and shift.

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:16.530
With our ILPS next hop, we
have made it very seamless

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:20.460
and scalable integration
of these appliances

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:22.950
by our bump-in-the-wire
mechanisms.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:27.030
What we have done is gone ahead
and enhanced that with three

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:29.500
additional enhancements.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:33.950
The first one is support
for symmetric hashing.

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:38.480
When you have these multiple
stateful firewall appliances

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.670
and you have
bidirectional traffic,

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:42.710
you need to ensure
that the return

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:46.910
traffic hits the same
instance on the way back.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:50.000
You typically do this by
configuring [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.750
But there's a lot of operational
toil associated with it.

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:57.620
But now, by using symmetric
hashing with our Layer 4

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:01.550
internal load balancer
for bidirectional flows,

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:06.600
you no longer have that
constraint and toil.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.550
The second enhancement
that we've done

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:12.210
is we've enabled all
protocols to flow

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:16.140
through ILB as next hop,
not just TCP and UDP.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.920
So now, by allowing
ICMP packets through,

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:22.530
we'll be able to support
ping and trace routes

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:26.630
so that you can better
troubleshoot your flows.

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:28.580
Last, but not the
least, we've also

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.940
added tagging support
for your load balancers.

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:35.210
What this effectively does
is it allows you to group

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:39.680
a certain set of endpoints
with a tag and associate them

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:45.140
with an ILB and route your
traffic from those clients

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:48.390
specifically to that ILB.

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:51.030
The most common use case
has been regionalization,

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:54.990
where you can tag all
workloads in a specific region

00:18:54.990 --> 00:19:01.100
and associate them with that
ILB in that specific region.

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.730
We have a very compelling
roadmap of our load balancing

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:06.290
offerings and features.

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:09.350
Let me share some
of them with you.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:11.810
If you look at this
roadmap across the board,

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:16.778
we have multiple flavors and
features of our load balancing.

00:19:16.778 --> 00:19:18.320
What I'd like to
bring your attention

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.720
to is in the Security
column, you'll

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:25.790
see that we've made TLS 1.3
the default for our HTPS load

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:28.220
balancers.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:30.560
With perfect forward
secrecy, getting

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:33.500
rid of old, less
secure ciphers, we

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:36.200
made our solution more secure.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:39.350
By cutting down on the
handshakes and the setup

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:44.300
latency, we've resulted in
a much more optimal end user

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:46.040
experience.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.530
On the scaling front,
if you'll notice,

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:52.520
we've added support for
Layer 4 ILB [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:56.090
What this allows you to do is
to scale your back ends, more

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:59.720
than 255 of your
Layer 4 load balancer,

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.970
and, hence, scale out
your service much--

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:07.387
now let me hand it off to
Adam to wrap this session up.

00:20:07.387 --> 00:20:08.470
ADAM MICHELSON: Thank you.

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:09.730
Thanks so much, Babi.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:13.203
So I wanted to thank all of
